{"qid":"Q10914393","names":["Neo-Assyrian Empire"],"title":"Neo-Assyrian Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Neo-Assyrian Empire arose from the revival of Assyrian power under Adad-nirari II (r. 911–891 BC) and reached its greatest territorial extent under Tiglath-Pileser III (r. 744–727 BC), Sargon II (r. 721–705 BC), and Esarhaddon (r. 680–669 BC), who conquered Egypt in 671 BC; the dataset attests it from 900 to 601 BC. A militarized bureaucratic state using systematic deportation to suppress revolts, Assyria created the first empire to govern the entire Fertile Crescent continuously. The empire collapsed rapidly after 627 BC: a Babylonian-Median coalition sacked Nineveh in 612 BC, and the last Assyrian army was defeated at Harran in 609 BC.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Neo-Assyrian borders are among the best-documented in the ancient Near East, attested by royal annals, tribute lists, administrative cuneiform tablets from Nineveh and Nimrud, and Eponym Chronicles; frontier zones in the Zagros, Anatolia, and Egypt are somewhat less precise.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Neo-Assyrian Empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Neo-Assyrian-Empire"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Neo-Assyrian Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-Assyrian_Empire"}],"britannica":"place/Neo-Assyrian-Empire","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Neo-Assyrian Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Adad-nirari II","nativeName":"𒀭𒅎𒊺𒋀","roman":"Adad-nērārī II","start":-911,"end":-891,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Tukulti-Ninurta II","nativeName":"𒄭𒀭𒛱𒀭𒛱","roman":"Tukult